[{:db/id "q-334937/field-1/2a8fe2160e75d8f4c8b3cecfdbe4ecc6f6616fb944f14050f02a241b341f7c0c" :answer/id #uuid "7d01aeec-7d6a-5e3c-bc6f-930ea3caafad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4t^2+12t^5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334937/field-1/d4bc5925d4b339a4faf3e1bbde5fd258378fb4ca65cafb5cfcdfbdfb10eed70e" :answer/id #uuid "b9fb68e4-e292-53f8-a493-4e4ef07e4a42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4t^2+6t^5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334937/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc22ace5-3daf-5a2b-b007-a1c8b707bcf3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334937/field-1/2a8fe2160e75d8f4c8b3cecfdbe4ecc6f6616fb944f14050f02a241b341f7c0c" :answer-field/choices ["q-334937/field-1/2a8fe2160e75d8f4c8b3cecfdbe4ecc6f6616fb944f14050f02a241b341f7c0c" "q-334937/field-1/d4bc5925d4b339a4faf3e1bbde5fd258378fb4ca65cafb5cfcdfbdfb10eed70e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334937" :question/id #uuid "dd7b44a3-6538-5678-b604-afe7ca773072" :question/math-academy-id "q-334937" :question/problem "Given that $Q(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${Q}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${Q}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${Q}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nQ(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+0+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334937/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334937"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334972/field-1/7da45e43d68636a1c0be98a4e08f8665107c0c9c41cca908ea82d06387391211" :answer/id #uuid "670786da-dad7-5799-a1c5-50cf557b21f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+e^t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186355556 :answer-field/choices ["q-334972/field-1/7da45e43d68636a1c0be98a4e08f8665107c0c9c41cca908ea82d06387391211"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335252/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "52bff97b-d237-5101-b351-f70e38d15ff3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335252/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "72547652-05c5-564c-9e68-1ce3e6081bc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335252/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "c2033edc-46d0-5266-ab71-615b97de26c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335252/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "00c9eb58-0f77-54ed-b99a-75d7ea415170" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335252/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "56d65fc7-222b-57df-af18-d5f179328795" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335252/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dca1d5cf-effe-5489-9ee3-4eb786f87d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335252/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-335252/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335252/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-335252/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-335252/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-335252/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335252" :question/id #uuid "9642dfed-e959-50ee-bb3a-bb0e4c6f718b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335252" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335252/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335252"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
